@@ -64,6 +66,45 @@ user commit unaware that open findings exist.
 - `--fix`: after reporting, apply fixes for the confirmed findings directly to the working
   tree, then re-run the affected checks (typecheck/tests) to confirm the fix didn't break
   anything. Do not silently fix — tell the user what was changed and why.
+- `--commit`: after the review (and the fixes, if `--fix` was also passed), commit the reviewed
+  diff locally — see Step 5.
+
+## Step 5 — Commit (only with `--commit`)
+
+Run this step **only** when `--commit` was explicitly passed in this invocation. Without it,
+never commit — not when the user says "revisar antes de commitar", not when this skill is
+preloaded into a reviewer subagent, not because the review came back clean.
+
+**Gates** — commit only if all three hold. If any fails, do not commit; say which gate failed
+and why, and leave the working tree as it is for the user to decide:
+
+1. **Something to commit** — the reviewed diff is not empty (whitespace/line-ending-only
+   changes count as empty). If it is, say there was nothing to commit.
+2. **No open findings** — every confirmed finding was fixed. Without `--fix`, any confirmed
+   finding blocks the commit. Uncertain findings (`high`+) don't block, but list them.
+3. **Project checks pass** — read the reviewed project's `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` (root and
+   `.claude/`) for the checks it requires before committing (e.g. typecheck, tests, build) and
+   run **all** of them in full, after the fixes — not just the affected ones. A failing check
+   blocks the commit: name it and quote the relevant error lines. Never skip, loosen or edit a
+   check to make it pass. If the project declares no checks, say so in the report (and which
+   checks, if any, you ran anyway) — don't commit silently.
+
+**What goes in and how:**
+
+- The whole reviewed diff — the original work plus the fixes, not only the fixes.
+- Stage by explicit path, only the files in the review scope: `git add -- <paths>`, then
+  `git commit -m "<message>" -- <paths>` so anything else already staged stays out. Never
+  `git add -A`, `git add .` or `git commit -a` — other agents may be working in the same tree.
+  Never stage `.env` files, credentials or secrets, even if they are in the diff.
+- Message in Conventional Commits (`type(scope): summary`), describing the change itself (not
+  the review), in the language of the repo's history (`git log -n 10 --format=%s`).
+- No `Co-Authored-By` trailer unless the project's `.claude/settings.json` sets
+  `attribution.commit`.
+- Commit on the current branch. No push, no new branch, no `--amend`, no `--no-verify`; don't
+  `stash`/`reset`/`checkout`/`clean` anything. If a hook rejects the commit, report it.
+
+**Report** the commit hash and message (`git log -1 --format="%h %s"`), or the gate that
+stopped it.
 
 ## Rules
 
